<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Province of Pennsylvania</h1><p class="st-meta">1681 CE – 1776 CE · capital Philadelphia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">William Penn's Quaker colony, a 'holy experiment' in religious freedom whose capital Philadelphia became the largest city in British America.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1681 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-england">Charles II</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a> a vast tract west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a> to settle a debt owed to Penn's father. Penn planned a refuge for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> and other persecuted groups, issued a Frame of Government in 1682, laid out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> on a grid and, by tradition, made a treaty of friendship with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shackamaxon">Shackamaxon</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Charter of Privileges of 1701 created a single elected assembly and guaranteed freedom of worship to all who believed in God. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a>, Germans, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germantown-philadelphia">Germantown</a> in 1683, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotch-irish-americans">Scots-Irish</a> settlers poured in, and grain exports made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> prosperous. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">Penn</a> heirs remained proprietors, and the Walking Purchase of 1737 cheated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> of land. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a> made Philadelphia a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>, science and civic institutions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-congress">Continental Congress</a>, which declared independence there in 1776. That year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> adopted a radical democratic constitution, ending proprietary rule, and became a state of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/province-of-pennsylvania.jpg" alt="Province of Pennsylvania" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: AnonMoos, based on image by Zscout370, AnonMoos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_ensign_of_Great_Britain_(1707%E2%80%931800,_square_canton).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1681 CE – 1776 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Philadelphia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William Penn, Thomas Penn, John Penn</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Quakerism and other Protestant churches</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pennsylvania (colony)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1728&amp;focus=province-of-pennsylvania" aria-label="Province of Pennsylvania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2829 748 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Province of Pennsylvania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3149 1004L4970 663M3149 1004L4997 651M3149 1004L2570 1008M3149 1004L3136 1013"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4970 663h0M4997 651h0M2570 1008h0M3136 1013h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3149 1004h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3149 1004h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Philadelphia. <a href="/atlas?year=1728&amp;focus=province-of-pennsylvania">On the atlas